    lesson: "Medical imaging measures physical signals and reconstructs or maps them into visual representations. Radiography and CT use transmitted X-rays; MRI uses radiofrequency signals from nuclei in a strong magnetic field; ultrasound records reflected acoustic waves; nuclear medicine detects radiation emitted by administered tracers. The image is therefore a measurement conditioned by acquisition settings, physics, reconstruction, and display—not a direct view of anatomy. Matching the modality to a question requires understanding what was measured, what safety considerations apply, and what artifacts or blind spots may occur.",
