      lesson(5, "Computed Tomography (CT)", "CT acquires multiple X-ray projections around the patient and computationally reconstructs cross-sectional images or a volume. In idealized CT, a reconstructed value relates to a linear attenuation coefficient μ relative to water: HU = 1000(μ − μwater)/μwater. HU are affected by calibration, energy, reconstruction, and artifacts. CT is fast and offers strong spatial detail, but uses ionizing radiation; protocol selection and dose optimization are clinical responsibilities.", "computed tomography CT image reconstruction Hounsfield units medical physics lecture", { formula: "HU = 1000 × (μ − μwater)/μwater; μ is the material attenuation estimate and μwater the water reference.", principles: ["Acquire angular projection measurements.", "Reconstruct cross-sectional attenuation estimates.", "Display with a window and level appropriate to the task."], comparison: "CT is fast and excels at many high-contrast and emergency applications; MRI avoids ionizing radiation and provides different soft-tissue contrast but has different access, timing, and safety constraints.", misconceptions: ["CT numbers are not universal tissue labels.", "Windowing changes display, not the underlying acquisition."] }),
      lesson(6, "Magnetic Resonance Tomography (MRI)", "MRI aligns nuclear magnetization using a strong static field, perturbs it with radiofrequency pulses, and detects emitted signals as spins relax. Gradient fields encode spatial location; reconstruction maps sampled frequency data (k-space) into images. T1, T2, proton density, diffusion, and other sequence choices emphasize different properties. MRI uses no ionizing X-rays, but strong-field, implant, projectile, acoustic-noise, and RF-heating safety screening is essential.", "MRI physics T1 T2 k-space magnetic resonance imaging university lecture", { formula: "The Larmor relation is f₀ = γB₀; f₀ is resonant frequency, γ the gyromagnetic ratio, and B₀ the static field.", principles: ["A strong field establishes net magnetization.", "RF pulses excite spins; relaxation produces measurable signals.", "Gradient encoding and reconstruction map signals to space."], advantages: "Flexible soft-tissue contrast and no ionizing radiation.", limitations: "Longer acquisitions in many workflows; motion, field inhomogeneity, implants, and sequence dependence matter.", misconceptions: ["MRI signal intensity is not a single universal tissue scale.", "Non-ionizing does not mean risk-free."] }),
      lesson(7, "Ultrasound", "Ultrasound sends high-frequency acoustic pulses and records echoes reflected or scattered at boundaries. Time delay estimates depth; echo amplitude, transducer geometry, and beamforming contribute to the displayed image. Acoustic impedance Z = ρc, where ρ is density and c sound speed; a mismatch contributes to reflection. Ultrasound is real-time, portable, and does not use ionizing radiation, but depends on acoustic windows and operator technique.", "ultrasound physics acoustic impedance piezoelectric transducer medical imaging lecture", { formula: "Z = ρc; Z is acoustic impedance, ρ density, and c sound speed in the medium.", principles: ["Transducer elements emit pulses and detect returning echoes.", "Echo time relates to depth under an assumed sound speed.", "Doppler methods estimate motion-related frequency shifts."], advantages: "Portable, real-time, and no ionizing radiation.", limitations: "Operator dependence, limited acoustic windows, and reduced penetration or image quality in some conditions.", misconceptions: ["Ultrasound images are not photographs.", "Absence of ionizing radiation does not remove all device or workflow considerations."] }),
      lesson(8, "Nuclear Medicine and Molecular Imaging", "Nuclear medicine images radiotracer distribution and physiological processes rather than only anatomy. A radiopharmaceutical emits detectable radiation; gamma cameras form planar images, while SPECT and PET acquire multiple views for tomographic reconstruction. PET commonly detects paired annihilation photons following positron emission. Spatial resolution, attenuation, scatter, counting statistics, tracer kinetics, and patient-specific context affect images.", "nuclear medicine PET SPECT molecular imaging physics lecture", { formula: "For ideal radioactive decay, N(t) = N₀e^(−λt); λ is decay constant and half-life T½ = ln(2)/λ.", principles: ["A tracer follows a biological pathway.", "Detectors measure emitted photons.", "Reconstruction estimates spatial tracer distribution."], advantages: "Can reveal functional or molecular information.", limitations: "Uses ionizing radiotracers; spatial resolution and count statistics differ from many anatomical modalities.", comparison: "CT/MRI often emphasize anatomy and structure; PET/SPECT primarily map tracer activity and may be combined with anatomical imaging.", misconceptions: ["A tracer distribution map is not equivalent to a direct anatomical photograph."] })
